# iknowwhatyoudid — credentials
#
# Copy to `credentials.toml` beside your config.toml and restrict it to your account.
# This file is the ONLY place a secret belongs. The tool never prints one, never writes
# one to the database, and never logs one.
#
# Two different questions are asked of this file:
#
#   * "is this credential name known?"  — every source asks this, and the answer
#     reveals nothing, so a file other accounts can read is only a warning.
#   * "what is this value?"             — only a client secret is read this way, and
#     a file other accounts can read is refused outright.

# --- a Gmail account -------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Google issues a client secret alongside the client ID when you create an OAuth client
# of type "Desktop app". Its token endpoint refuses to exchange a code without it.
#
# It is NOT the client_id. The client_id is public — it appears in every sign-in URL —
# and belongs in config.toml. This is the other half, and it belongs only here.

# [credential.REPLACE-gmail-credential-name]
# client_secret = "REPLACE-with-the-client-secret-from-your-OAuth-client"

# --- a Microsoft 365 account -----------------------------------------------------------
#
# Needs no secret at all: the sign-in uses PKCE, which exists precisely so that a desktop
# application does not have to hold one. The entry must still exist, because a source
# names it and `ikwyd sources validate` checks the name is known — but it stays empty.
#
# A client_secret placed here is never sent, and `sources validate` says so.

# [credential.REPLACE-outlook-credential-name]
